6e4e2dc013","fae88dd7a1f11b0a","fbc9d2bb31585568","fbf502941001b519","fcdac5a3ae909709","fd3b55a3bab36cdf"],"groups":["abd0a669fd0aa4e7","e06bc88d96e4590c","fb40b8a810742773","ab34dc9bc3556bbc","6514bceeb9393d01","1c3bafbdefd2803d","566f9654e67bd0ff","caf2adf4e4eb54de"],"findings":"**Mismatches**\n\n1. **`decidedShort` (group `rail`) does not match its own intent, and this is verified.** The intent is \"nothing unless it found issues\". The code returns `''` only when `latest.verdict === 'clean'`. A review with no `VERDICT:` line has `verdict` undefined, so it gets a rail suffix. I built a scratch repo with a review whose findings are \"Looks odd, not sure.\" (no verdict line). Page data gave `verdict: 'unknown'` on the stage and `entry.verdict` undefined, with `decisions: []` and `summary: \"none yet\"`. The rail therefore reads \"reviewed · current · undecided\" for a review that found nothing. **Verified.** Fix: return `''` unless `latest.verdict === 'issues'`.\n\n2. **The rail suffix reads only the newest review, but the rail's verdict text is aggregated across reviews.** `reviewShort(r)` uses `verdict(s)`, which takes the latest verdict per part of the commit. `decidedShort` uses `r.entries[0]` alone. If an older review found issues on a line and a newer clean review covers only other lines, the row says \"issues found\" while `decidedShort` returns `''` and says nothing about the decisions on the issues review. **Read**, not run. The author's \"how far they got\" claim is weaker than stated in that case.\n\n3. **Minor wording mismatch on `rail`.** The intent says \"decided (complete)\", but the code emits ` · complete` with no count. The test asserts that exact form, so the code is self-consistent, but the intent text does not match. **Verified** by the test assertion (`/ · complete$/`).\n\n4. **The `docs` README sentence is a restatement.** The new line \"Likewise, decisions follow their reviews through `adopt` and `carry` only in versions that know them\" repeats the existing Known cost paragraph. It is not wrong, but it adds nothing. **Read.**\n\n**Misgraded necessity**\n\n5. **`docs` [required] is overgraded.** The TODO removal is housekeeping, and the README edit documents behaviour. Neither is needed for the feature to work. Supporting, or mechanical, fits better. Note that the README now claims \"the latest 200 decisions\" and the rail behaviour, so it has to stay in step with the code.\n\n6. **The `MAX_PAGE_DECISIONS` cap inside `data` [required] is defensive.** Nobody asked for the cap. It is a reasonable guard against unbounded page size, so I would label it defensive and keep it. It is bundled into a required group, which hides that.\n\n**Removal candidates**\n\n7. None of the groups is a clear removal. `rail` and `style` are small and serve the stated intent. The cap in point 6 could be dropped at some cost, since a review with thousands of decisions would bloat the page. The test-side `process.chdir` in `data()` is a side effect, not a removal candidate.\n\n**Simplifications**\n\n8. Move the rail suffix into `data.ts` as a precomputed field, such as `railNote`. Then the client does not re-derive state, and the verdict bug in point 1 lives in one place with the verdict logic (`verdict()` in `src/reviews.ts`). The client also would not need `latest.verdict`.\n\n9. In `pageReviews`, `entry.verdict` exists only so `decidedShort` can test for clean. Once point 8 lands, `verdict` and possibly `decisionCount` may be simpler to keep on the entry only where the pane needs them.\n\n**Missing risks**\n\n10. **Extra git calls per commit (read).** `readDecisions(sha)` runs `git notes show` and then calls `readReviews(sha)`, which runs `git notes show` again. `pageReviews` already runs `reviewStatus`, which reads reviews. Each commit in a range now does roughly two more git processes. This is a cost on large ranges, not a correctness problem.\n\n11. **The pane shows \"Decisions · none yet\" for any non-clean review,** inc